                <pb facs="0258" n="130" xml:id="pg130"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 89.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Hall</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-06-19"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 19,
                        1885</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Bayard telegraphs Mr. Hall that the deepest concern is felt by this
                    Government in the welfare of the Central American States, and it is earnestly
                    hoped that force will not be resorted to; that mutual safety and welfare can
                    only be restored and maintained by the exercise of reason and justice; and
                    instructs Mr. Hall to use his best offices to bring about a peaceable
                    settlement. The Department is pleased to learn that he is within telegraphic
                    communication, and so situated as to be enabled to exert his influence toward
                    bringing this unhappy contest to a close.</p>
            </div>
